**Read:** [Phases, diffusion and solidification — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Phases-and-Diffusion-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_4HHCB32507KCQ5FPHGT4JD5GTB), Diffusion, random walks and interdiffusion; [Materials kinetics — MIT 3.205 and 3.21](../sources/Kinetic-Processes-in-Materials-MIT.lingua#r_2BZVQ9NG9Y2XTF4QG0H3JYYS16), 3.21 lectures 1–4 and 10–14.

For independent isotropic jumps of length a occurring at total rate Γ in d dimensions, ⟨r²⟩=Γa²t=2dDt, giving D=Γa²/(2d). Correlated jumps, anisotropy and unequal populations change the relation. The rate convention must specify whether Γ is total or per direction.

Near suitable equilibrium conditions, a flux can be written in terms of a chemical-potential gradient and mobility. A concentration gradient is only a simpler driving description when thermodynamic factors and other fields permit it. Uphill concentration diffusion can lower total free energy in a separating mixture.

**Work:** Derive the mean-square displacement from independent increments. Reconcile a per-neighbor rate with a total rate. Explain how an electric field, stress or activity gradient changes the driving potential, and specify a frame before comparing fluxes.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
